.\" fold.1 -- maux's fold(1), in the V7 man(1) shape. An original
.\" page (this project's own text), so it carries no third-party
.\" notice -- MIT like the code, same posture as cut.1/head.1.
.\" Rendered at build time to build/man/FOLD.TXT and staged on the
.\" play image as /usr/man/FOLD: `more /usr/man/FOLD`.
.TH FOLD 1 "September 14, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
fold \- break long lines
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fold
[
.B \-bs
] [
.BI \-w " width"
] [ file ] ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Fold
breaks each line of each named
.I file
(standard input default) so that no output line exceeds
.I width
columns (80 by default), writing a newline at each break.
.PP
In the default mode a tab advances to the next multiple-of-8
column and a backspace moves back one column (never past 0); a
tab that would push the column past
.I width
forces a break before the tab rather than after it, unless the
line is already empty, in which case the overshoot is accepted
rather than looping forever.
.B \-b
counts
.I width
in raw bytes instead, with no tab or backspace handling at all.
.PP
.B \-w " width"
sets the width explicitly.
.PP
.B \-s
breaks after the last
.I <blank>
.RI ( <space>
or
.IR <tab> )
within the width instead of exactly at the width, provided the
line has one; a line with no blank in range breaks at the width
regardless.
.PP
Multiple files are folded independently: the column state
starts fresh at each operand, and an unterminated final line is
flushed as it stands, without a synthesized newline \(em so two
files, the first ending without one, may appear to run
together on one output line.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/FOLD  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
cut(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0; 1 if a file named on the command line could
not be opened \(em that file is skipped, a diagnostic naming it
is written to standard error, and the remaining operands are
still processed; 2 for a usage error, including
.B \-w 0
or a width over 120.
.SH BUGS
The width is capped at 120 columns.
